<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Community</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French Community was an association created by de Gaulle's constitution of 1958 to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, offering colonies autonomy within a French framework. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> immediately chose independence instead, and within a few years most African members became independent, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-community.jpg" alt="French Community" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_the_French_Community.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>De Gaulle's <b>French Community</b> of 1958 offered autonomy within a French framework; Guinea under Sékou Touré rejected it outright and took immediate independence, and within two years the remaining African territories had followed, dissolving the entire administrative edifice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the referendum of 28 September 1958 on Charles de Gaulle’s <b>French Community</b>, Guinea under Ahmed Sékou Touré voted no and became independent on 2 October 1958; the other territories voted yes and became autonomous republics within the Community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1958 the four territories became autonomous republics within the <b>French Community</b> and the federation was dissolved, its members becoming independent in August 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>Houphouet-Boigny, who also served as a minister in Paris, led the territory to autonomy within the <b>French Community</b> in 1958 and to independence on 7 August 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-ivory-coast">French Ivory Coast</a></li><li><blockquote>Senegal became an autonomous republic within the <b>French Community</b> in 1958, joined the Mali Federation in 1959, and became fully independent on 20 August 1960 under Leopold Sedar Senghor.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1958 Gabon became an autonomous republic within the <b>French Community</b> and gained full independence on 17 August 1960 under Léon M'ba, keeping close economic and military ties with France and Libreville as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon (French colony)</a></li></ul></section>
